                <h2>Why Do Top-ups, Ad Spend, and Internal Balances Often Fail to Reconcile?</h2>
<p><strong>Question: Finance records the amount paid, while the media team reports insufficient account balances. Where is the discrepancy usually coming from?</strong></p>
<p>The money is rarely “missing.” The issue is usually caused by three inconsistent reporting bases. The internal ledger records the actual amount paid, the ad platform records the credited currency, and spend is calculated using the platform’s billing cycle, time zone, and exchange rate.</p>
<p>Create a unified advertising funds reconciliation table containing the top-up reference, payer, credited account, credited currency, platform amount, actual payment, exchange rate, top-up date, account balance, spend, refunds, and invoice number.</p>
<p>Reconciliation should verify each item individually:</p>
<ol>
<li>Whether the payment was credited successfully;</li>
<li>Whether the credit currency matches the spending currency;</li>
<li>Whether platform fees, temporary holds, or promotional credits are recorded separately;</li>
<li>Whether spend from the previous month remains in the current account balance;</li>
<li>Whether invoices include promotional credits, taxes, or refund adjustments.</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p>If the difference between internal and platform balances cannot be explained by exchange rates, fees, credits, time zones, or refunds, pause large top-ups until the discrepancy is resolved.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Which Exchange Rate Should Be Used for Multi-Currency Accounts?</h2>
<p><strong>Question: Should USD, EUR, and JPY accounts use the month-start exchange rate or the payment-date rate?</strong></p>
<p>Use a dual-rate approach. Convert top-ups using the actual payment or bank settlement date. Convert platform spend using the settlement rate shown in the platform invoice. Then revalue the month-end balance using the company’s standard closing rate.</p>
<p>Where the platform provides a clear billing currency and settlement rate, use that information for account-level reconciliation. The general ledger may follow the company’s accounting policy, but media reports should preserve original currencies so that exchange-rate movements are not mistaken for changes in advertising performance.</p>
<p>For example, a utility App tops up a USD account with $10,000 and spends $8,600 during the month, leaving $1,400. If Finance applies the payment-date rate while the media team applies the month-end rate, a small difference is inevitable. Preserve the original-currency difference and document its cause rather than revising historical spend.</p>
<h2>Which Date Should Be Used to Match Spend with Invoices?</h2>
<p><strong>Question: The media team reports spend in the account time zone, while Finance closes the books by calendar month. How should the two be aligned?</strong></p>
<p>Keep account time zones for campaign analysis, but establish a separate billing attribution rule for finance. A practical internal standard is the Beijing time calendar month, with the following mappings:</p>
<ul>
<li>Spend date: based on actual delivery records;</li>
<li>Settlement date: based on the date the platform confirms the charge;</li>
<li>Invoice date: recorded as the issue date and not automatically treated as the spend month;</li>
<li>Refund date: offset against the original transaction month to prevent duplicate revenue or expense recognition.</li>
</ul>
<p>Create a bridge for cross-month transactions. Spend incurred in the previous month but settled this month is recognized as a current-month receivable. A reversal processed this month for a charge settled last month is recorded as a current-month adjustment. This explains differences between invoice totals and delivery reports without altering actual advertising data.</p>
<h2>What If the Payer, Advertiser, and Invoice Entity Do Not Match?</h2>
<p><strong>Question: Different companies pay for accounts in different regions and receive invoices under different entities. Can these accounts still be consolidated?</strong></p>
<p>They can be managed centrally, but they should not be recorded as one transaction. For each account group, clearly map the payer, contracting entity, advertiser entity, and invoice recipient. Add the account’s country, media platform, and currency.</p>
<p>A unified project code may be used, but each entity must retain separate records for top-ups, spend, refunds, and invoices. For payments made on behalf of another entity, define the internal transfer price, the party bearing exchange-rate risk, and the required supporting documents. If the invoice recipient cannot be corrected, do not continue using an incorrect entity as a permanent workaround. Reissue the invoice according to the contract and local tax requirements.</p>
<p>The key issue is not merely whether an invoice has been received. It is whether the invoice accurately reflects the entity responsible for the advertising activity. Entity mismatches can affect accounting, tax treatment, consolidated reporting, and compliance reviews.</p>
<h2>In What Order Should Monthly Reconciliation Be Performed?</h2>
<p><strong>Question: With many accounts, how can teams avoid discovering balance problems only at month-end?</strong></p>
<p>Do not wait until invoices are issued. A daily monitoring, weekly verification, and monthly close process is more effective.</p>
<h3>Daily Monitoring</h3>
<p>Check whether payments have been credited, whether balances are below the warning threshold, and whether there are abnormal charges or account restrictions. Set balance thresholds based on the trailing seven-day average daily spend and the top-up settlement time rather than using the same amount for every account.</p>
<h3>Weekly Verification</h3>
<p>Match top-up transactions, platform spend, wallet balances, and bank statements at the account level. Investigate promotional credits, taxes, refunds, and time-zone-related differences.</p>
<h3>Monthly Close</h3>
<p>Aggregate spend using the unified monthly attribution rule, reconcile platform settlement amounts with invoice details, and prepare an account-level variance report. Review and approve it jointly by media buying, procurement, and finance teams. Classify each variance as pending credit, currency conversion, timing difference, fees, promotional credits, refunds, or entity mismatch.</p>
<p>The recommended end state consists of three linked reports: a daily account funds report, a monthly spend-to-balance bridge, and an invoice-to-entity mapping table. Connecting these reports through standardized account IDs and transaction references creates a closed-loop process from top-up to invoice.</p>
